# Build output. dist/ is the real one; dist-* are scratch builds used when more than one
# thing is being built at once, and must never be committed.
dist/
dist-*/
*.tsbuildinfo

# The hermetic browser, fetched into the repo by PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH. Large, and pinned
# by revision in renderer/vendor/VERSIONS.md rather than by being checked in.
.browsers/

node_modules/

# Scratch render output and trajectories. goldens/ and examples/ are deliberately NOT ignored.
out/

# Render-metrics, env-model, judge and PNG caches (keyed by content hash; regenerable).
.cache/

# Corpus pixels. corpus/manifest.jsonl and corpus/readings/ ARE tracked — they are the evidence,
# they are small, and a provenance claim has to be checkable from a clone. The images are neither:
# every one is refetchable from the `image_url` on its manifest row and verifiable against the
# sha256 on the same row, which is what `corpus verify` does. Measured across all 41,511 of
# Cleveland's CC0 records the mean web derivative is 356KB, so tracking them would put 10.7GB in git
# at a 30,000-work corpus. Untracked at 50 works, while it was still free to do.
corpus/images/
corpus/failures.jsonl

# The candidate pool: a quarter of a million rows across three museums, ~110MB, regenerable by
# re-running `corpus metadata`. It is a fact about three public APIs rather than about this corpus.
# The argument for tracking the manifest was that the manifest is small; a file that is not small
# does not inherit that argument by having a similar name. What the pool would have justified — what
# was available, and why these works were chosen out of it — is in corpus/selection.json, which IS
# tracked.
corpus/pool.jsonl

# Env / secrets
.env
*.env
!.env.example

# OS
.DS_Store

# The atlas and its viewer. Both are recomputed from `corpus/manifest.jsonl` in about three seconds
# by `corpus atlas`, and together they are 6.5MB of derived numbers. The manifest is the evidence;
# these are a reading of it.
corpus/atlas.json
corpus/atlas.html
